<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: LicenseRef-Chromaticity-Proprietary
  SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Chromaticity

  macOS Info.plist override merged into the bundled .app/Contents/Info.plist
  by Tauri 2's bundler per `bundle.macOS.infoPlist` in `tauri.conf.json`.

  Phase-4a Wave 1c-β Phase 2 PR 2 wires `NSCameraUsageDescription` for the
  camera adapter (ADR-020 §5). The TCC permission subsystem reads this key
  from the bundled .app/Contents/Info.plist; missing key skips the consent
  dialog entirely (the OS denies access without surfacing a UI), per
  `docs/54-wave-1c-beta-research-lessons.md` Phase-2 lesson 5.

  Audio capture (ADR-022) declares two TCC usage strings:
    - NSMicrophoneUsageDescription gates the default-input (microphone /
      line-in) cpal capture path.
    - NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription gates the zero-install system-audio
      LOOPBACK path (cpal 0.17 Core Audio process taps, macOS 14.6+;
      `engine/audio::try_system_audio_loopback`). That consent surfaces as
      the purple recording indicator, NOT the orange mic dot.
  Both pair with `com.apple.security.device.audio-input` in
  `release-eng/entitlements.plist`. A missing usage string makes the OS
  deny access without a UI, so a denied / sub-14.6 system-audio request
  degrades to the default input rather than prompting (there is no public
  API to preflight tap consent).

  The release-validation script `scripts/check-camera-info-plist.sh`
  asserts the camera key survives Tauri's plist merge into the bundled output.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Chromaticity uses the camera to provide live video input for visual scenes selected by the operator.</string>
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Chromaticity uses the microphone or selected audio input to drive audio-reactive visuals.</string>
  <key>NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Chromaticity captures the audio your computer is playing so visuals can react to it, without installing a virtual audio device.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
